### What happened?

`models.generateImages()` sends the built-in OpenRouter model `openai/gpt-image-2.5-flare` to `/api/v1/chat/completions`. OpenRouter returns HTTP 404:
```text
openai/gpt-image-2.5-flare is an image generation model and cannot be used with the chat/completions endpoint. Use the /api/v1/images endpoint instead.
```
Pi returns `stopReason: "error"` with no images.

### Steps to reproduce

With `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` set, save as `repro.mts` and run `node repro.mts`:
```typescript
import { builtinModels } from "@earendil-works/pi-ai/providers/all";

const models = builtinModels();
const model = models.getModelOfType(
  "image", "openrouter", "openai/gpt-image-2.5-flare"
)!;

const result = await models.generateImages(model, {
  input: [{ type: "text", text: "A red circle on a white background" }],
}, {
  apiKey: process.env.OPENROUTER_API_KEY,
  maxRetries: 0,
});

console.log(result.stopReason, result.errorMessage);
```

### Expected behavior

Pi should use the dedicated Images API for this model and return the generated image.

### Version

1.1.0


### ai disclosure
I used coding agent to help find this bug/write this issue, but I reviewed and reproduced the bug by myself.

### What happened?

`CodemodeSandbox.execute('return 42;')` fails when its host runs with `node --watch`. This breaks codemode in SDK applications using Node's development watcher.

The result is a sandbox error: `Sandbox bridge broken: unknown message from the worker. The script may have modified built-ins such as a prototype's toJSON.`

Node sends dependency notifications such as `{ "watch:import": ["file:///.../worker.ts"] }` on the worker message channel. The host treats these as broken codemode messages and terminates execution. This is related to nodejs/node#65044.

### Steps to reproduce

In a source checkout with dependencies installed, save this as `repro.mjs` at the repository root:

```js
import { CodemodeSandbox } from './packages/codemode/src/index.ts';
const sandbox = new CodemodeSandbox();
try {
  console.log(await sandbox.execute('return 42;'));
} finally {
  await sandbox.close();
}
```

`node repro.mjs` returns `ok: true, value: 42`; `node --watch repro.mjs` returns the error above. This invokes the sandbox directly, without extensions or model requests.

### Expected behavior

Both runs return 42. I fixed this in #10726, with regression tests. The fix ignores only well-formed `watch:import` and `watch:require` notifications and keeps rejecting unknown messages.

### Version

Main at `f1b2e77f5b13b2a199b1052cb79c235451afe7d7`, Node v24.18.1, macOS arm64.

### What happened?

On a session with no system message yet, a run started by `sendCustomMessage(msg, { triggerTurn: true })` (or `pi.sendMessage(..., { triggerTurn: true })` from an extension) sends its first request without the system prompt. `prepareNextTurnWithContext` adds it from the second request on, so a run that ends after one request never sees it, and the second request can't reuse the first one's cached prefix.

`prompt()` prepends the system message before starting the run ([L2105-L2107](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/abe508e1b89912adde45528136c3221eb69acdd7/packages/coding-agent/src/core/agent-session.ts#L2105-L2107)); the `triggerTurn` branch calls `_runAgentPrompt(appMessage)` directly ([L2314-L2319](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/abe508e1b89912adde45528136c3221eb69acdd7/packages/coding-agent/src/core/agent-session.ts#L2314-L2319)). [session-format.md](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/abe508e1b89912adde45528136c3221eb69acdd7/packages/coding-agent/docs/session-format.md#L80) says the first request persists it.

### Steps to reproduce

Faux-provider test in `packages/coding-agent/test/suite/`:

```ts
const harness = await createHarness({ tools: [echoTool], initialActiveToolNames: ["echo"] });
const prompts: string[] = [];
harness.setResponses([
	(context) => {
		prompts.push(getCurrentSystemPrompt(context.messages));
		return fauxAssistantMessage([fauxToolCall("echo", { text: "hi" })], { stopReason: "toolUse" });
	},
	(context) => {
		prompts.push(getCurrentSystemPrompt(context.messages));
		return fauxAssistantMessage("done");
	},
]);
await harness.session.sendCustomMessage({ customType: "task", content: "hello", display: true }, { triggerTurn: true });
// prompts.map((p) => p.length) -> [0, 1762]; with session.prompt("hello") -> [1762, 1762]
```

### Expected behavior

The first request carries the system prompt, as with `prompt()`.

Proposed fix: in that branch (including the deferred `_isEmittingAgentSettled` case), call `_preparePromptAndToolLoadout` and prepend its system message as `prompt()` does. I'd like to send the PR, with this repro as a regression test.

### Version

main @ 1cedd32 (1.1.0)

With @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent 1.1.0 on Node 24, an extension's `before_provider_headers` handler runs before `before_provider_request`, and the Codex header event does not include `Authorization` or `chatgpt-account-id`.

The event type documents the hook as running after request headers are assembled. An extension implementing Codex compaction cannot reuse the actual authenticated headers from this event.

A minimal observer (no header values logged):

```ts
export default function (pi) {
  pi.on("before_provider_headers", (event) => {
    const headers = new Headers(event.headers);
    console.error("headers", {
      authorizationPresent: headers.has("Authorization"),
      accountPresent: headers.has("chatgpt-account-id"),
    });
  });
  pi.on("before_provider_request", () => console.error("payload"));
}
```

Run one prompt with a signed-in `openai-codex` model. The header event comes first and reports both fields absent. The normal provider request succeeds.

In the installed source, `sdk.ts` routes `transformHeaders` into this event, while the Codex API implementation later adds its authorization and account headers in `buildBaseCodexHeaders`.

Could the documented hook receive the fully assembled transport headers, or could a separate supported hook expose those? An extension workaround is to obtain request auth through `ctx.modelRegistry.getApiKeyAndHeaders(model)` and derive the Codex account ID from the access-token claim, but that duplicates provider-specific header construction.

### What happened?

Two tasks that wait on each other ([`allSettled`](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/4c6fb7cfe8c538a668726f6f8b3554098c39faee/packages/durable/docs/spec.md?plain=1#L2089-L2094)) both stay [`waiting`](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/4c6fb7cfe8c538a668726f6f8b3554098c39faee/packages/durable/docs/spec.md?plain=1#L1585-L1591) forever, even after reopen. Nothing reports it: the tasks look like ordinary waiting work, the conversation never goes idle, and only [`abortTask`](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/4c6fb7cfe8c538a668726f6f8b3554098c39faee/packages/durable/docs/spec.md?plain=1#L868-L877) frees them. As tasks wait on subagents and other tasks, a cycle like this can come from ordinary task code.

[`#validateWait`](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/4c6fb7cfe8c538a668726f6f8b3554098c39faee/packages/durable/src/harness/scheduler.ts#L1003-L1025) already rejects a wait on the task itself or its owner chain, because those "could never finish first" ([§5.1](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/4c6fb7cfe8c538a668726f6f8b3554098c39faee/packages/durable/docs/spec.md?plain=1#L1735-L1737)). The same reason covers any cycle in the wait-for graph, where an edge T → U means "T can't finish before U". There are two kinds, and a cycle can mix them:

- T is `waiting` with U in its `on`
- U is live ordinary owned work of T, so T holds as [`completing`](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/4c6fb7cfe8c538a668726f6f8b3554098c39faee/packages/durable/docs/spec.md?plain=1#L1592-L1593) until U is done ([§5.5](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/4c6fb7cfe8c538a668726f6f8b3554098c39faee/packages/durable/docs/spec.md?plain=1#L2054-L2061), the nested-transaction rule)

But currently only the two shortest cycles are checked:

| cycle | today |
|---|---|
| a waits on a | rejected |
| a child waits on its owner | rejected |
| a waits on b, b waits on a | hangs |
| a cycle through a held owner (below) | hangs |

### Steps to reproduce

```
               owns (holds)
  orchestrator ────────────▶ worker
       ▲                       │ waits
       │ waits                 ▼
       └──────────────── reviewer
```

The less obvious case mixes the two, and each step is reasonable on its own:

1. An orchestrator spawns a worker task, then returns its result. The worker is still running, so the orchestrator holds as `completing` until it finishes.
2. A reviewer waits for the orchestrator's final result.
3. The worker waits for the reviewer's feedback before finishing.

None of the three can finish first, and all three hang. Longer plain cycles (x→y→z→x) hang too.

<details><summary>Tests (append to <code>test/harness-structured.test.ts</code>; both time out)</summary>

```ts
it("rejects a wait cycle", async () => {
	const { harness, root } = await openNodes();
	let a: TaskId | undefined;
	let b: TaskId | undefined;
	script("a", { run: (runtime, ctx) => runtime.commit(() => waitOn([b!], "allSettled"), ctx) });
	script("b", { run: (runtime, ctx) => runtime.commit(() => waitOn([a!], "allSettled"), ctx) });
	await root.commit(async (tx) => {
		a = await tx.createTask(Node, { name: "a" }, OWN_CONVERSATION);
		b = await tx.createTask(Node, { name: "b" }, OWN_CONVERSATION);
	}, context);
	const outcomes = await Promise.all([outcomeOf(harness, a!), outcomeOf(harness, b!)]);
	expect(outcomes.sort()).toEqual(["completed", "faulted"]);
	await harness.close(context);
});

it("rejects a wait cycle through a completing hold", async () => {
	const { harness, root } = await openNodes();
	const ids: Record<string, TaskId> = {};
	const reviewerWaits = deferred();

	// 1. The orchestrator spawns a worker, then returns its result in its next phase (the default `resume`). The worker
	//    is still running, so the orchestrator holds as `completing`: orchestrator → worker.
	script("orchestrator", {
		run: (runtime, ctx) =>
			runtime.commit(async (tx) => {
				ids.worker = await spawn(tx, runtime.taskId, "worker");
				return { status: "running", checkpoint: { phase: "resume", round: 1 } };
			}, ctx),
	});
	// 2. The reviewer waits for the orchestrator's final result: reviewer → orchestrator.
	script("reviewer", {
		run: async (runtime, ctx) => {
			await runtime.commit(() => waitOn([ids.orchestrator!], "allSettled"), ctx);
			reviewerWaits.resolve();
		},
	});
	// 3. The worker waits for the reviewer's feedback before finishing: worker → reviewer, closing the cycle.
	script("worker", {
		run: async (runtime, ctx) => {
			await reviewerWaits.promise;
			await runtime.commit(() => waitOn([ids.reviewer!], "allSettled"), ctx);
		},
	});

	ids.orchestrator = await start(root, "orchestrator");
	await until(async () => (await state(harness, ids.orchestrator!)).status === "completing");
	ids.reviewer = await start(root, "reviewer");

	// Today all three hang. Expected: the worker's wait is rejected, faulting it; then the orchestrator and reviewer finish.
	const outcomes = await Promise.all(
		["orchestrator", "worker", "reviewer"].map((name) => outcomeOf(harness, ids[name]!)),
	);
	expect(outcomes).toEqual(["completed", "faulted", "completed"]);
	await harness.close(context);
});
```
</details>

### Expected behavior

The wait that closes a cycle faults, like a wait on the owner already does. A check has to follow both kinds of edge: `on` edges alone miss the cycle above. Happy to send a fix.

Refs: Holt, ["Some Deadlock Properties of Computer Systems"](https://doi.org/10.1145/356603.356607), 1972 (wait-for graphs); Moss, [*Nested Transactions*](https://ftp.mirrorservice.org/sites/www.bitsavers.org/pdf/mit/lcs/tr/MIT-LCS-TR-0260.pdf), 1981.

### Version

1.0.1 ([4c6fb7c](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/commit/4c6fb7cfe8c538a668726f6f8b3554098c39faee))

### What happened?

In Pi 1.1.0, a still-running nested tool receives         
duplicate call IDs when it calls ctx.executeTool() before 
and after the parent agent run ends. The later call is    
also absent from the parent tool result_s audit record.

### Steps to reproduce

The attached extension reproduces this without    
other extensions loaded.

pi --version && pi --no-extensions --no-skills          
--no-prompt-templates --no-context-files --no-mcp         
--no-session --extension ./background-repro.ts --tools 
read,repro_start,repro_driver --thinking off --print "Call
repro_start exactly once, then finish without further tool
calls."

1.1.0                                                     
BACKGROUND_REPRO                                          
{"ids":["call_Hp6wdIEew1osk77R2P1QznEf|fc_01ef774355397681016ac9d2ab480087d2a8dfb9074ac639b2/1/1","call_Hp6wdIEew1osk77R2P1QznEf|fc_01ef774355397681016ac9d2ab480087d2a8dfb9074ac639b2/1/1"],"guardedReads":2,"duplicateIds":true,"driverError":false}

background-repro.ts:

import { Type } from "typebox";
import type { ExtensionAPI, ExtensionToolContext } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";

function gate() {
  let resolve!: () => void;
  const promise = new Promise<void>(done => { resolve = done; });
  return { promise, resolve };
}

export default function (pi: ExtensionAPI) {
  const ready = gate(), afterEnd = gate();
  const controller = new AbortController();
  const ids: string[] = [];
  let guardedReads = 0, started = false, reported = false;
  let driver: ReturnType<ExtensionToolContext["executeTool"]> | undefined;
  const reply = (text: string) => ({ content: [{ type: "text" as const, text }], details: {} });

  // Prevent all filesystem access. Both reads still pass through Pi's hooks.
  pi.on("tool_call", event => {
    if (event.toolName === "read") {
      guardedReads++;
      return { block: true, reason: "Blocked by reproduction guard" };
    }
  });

  pi.registerTool({
    name: "repro_driver", label: "Reproduction driver", exposure: "deferred",
    description: "Internal helper for repro_start.", parameters: Type.Object({}),
    async execute(_id, _args, signal, _update, ctx) {
      if (!started) throw new Error("Call repro_start first");
      const first = await ctx.executeTool("read", { path: "unused.txt" }, { signal });
      ids.push(first.toolCall.id);
      ready.resolve();
      await afterEnd.promise;
      signal?.throwIfAborted();
      const second = await ctx.executeTool("read", { path: "unused.txt" }, { signal });
      ids.push(second.toolCall.id);
      return reply("Driver finished");
    },
  });

  pi.registerTool({
    name: "repro_start", label: "Start reproduction", exposure: "model-only",
    description: "Run the background nested-call reproduction once, then finish the agent run.",
    parameters: Type.Object({}),
    async execute(_id, _args, _signal, _update, ctx) {
      if (started) throw new Error("Restart Pi to repeat this one-shot reproduction");
      started = true;
      driver = ctx.executeTool("repro_driver", {}, { signal: controller.signal });
      await Promise.race([
        ready.promise,
        driver.then(outcome => { throw new Error(`Driver ended before readiness: ${JSON.stringify(outcome)}`); }),
      ]);
      return reply("First read finished. End this agent run without calling more tools.");
    },
  });

  pi.on("agent_end", async (_event, ctx) => {
    if (!driver || reported) return;
    reported = true;
    // Pi clears its nested-call scopes before delivering this event.
    afterEnd.resolve();
    const outcome = await driver;
    const report = {
      ids, guardedReads,
      duplicateIds: ids.length === 2 && ids[0] === ids[1],
      driverError: outcome.isError,
    };
    pi.appendEntry("background-repro-result", report);
    console.error("BACKGROUND_REPRO " + JSON.stringify(report));
    if (ctx.hasUI) ctx.ui.notify(JSON.stringify(report), "info");
  });

  pi.on("session_shutdown", async () => {
    controller.abort();
    afterEnd.resolve();
    await driver;
  });
}

### Expected behavior

_No response_

### Version

1.1.0

prs.body:
## What Problem This Solves

When Copilot's usage endpoint returns malformed JSON with HTTP 200, the usage report shows an internal bounded-reader diagnostic instead of useful recovery guidance.

## Why This Change Was Made

The Copilot plugin normalizes only its existing labeled JSON parse and decode failures. The shared bounded reader keeps its 16 MiB limit, stream cancellation, fatal UTF-8 decoding, and transport-failure behavior. This changes no shared SDK contract and does not recover quota from malformed data; whether a wording-only diagnostic change is worth keeping remains a maintainer decision.

## User Impact

The usage report now tells operators that Copilot returned unreadable data and to try refreshing usage later. Healthy providers and valid Copilot quotas remain visible.

## Evidence

- Validation base: `2cbc6abdc23677cd3ff9c874b5e15887069c6602`; exact tested HEAD: `c4ba6cc7ddc9e07e5acae1caa5a4138b95a27415`.
- The same production status CLI and loopback HTTP fixture ran on the validation base and exact HEAD with an HTTP 200 body of `{`. The base printed the reader diagnostic; the exact HEAD printed the recovery guidance. The healthy Claude provider stayed visible in both runs.
- The exact-head valid-response negative control kept Copilot's Premium and Chat quotas visible. The harness redirected only allowlisted GitHub and Anthropic HTTP requests to localhost, used disposable auth/state, and made no external provider call.
- Focused validation: `pnpm test extensions/github-copilot/usage.test.ts src/agents/provider-http-errors.test.ts --maxWorkers=1` passed 44 tests across two Vitest shards in 25.25 seconds, including malformed JSON, invalid UTF-8, and transport-failure cases.

```text
$ PROOF_MODE=malformed PROOF_EXPECT_COPILOT_LINE='Copilot: github-copilot-usage: malformed JSON response' node proof-copilot-cli.mjs  # base 2cbc6abdc236
mode=malformed CLI exit=0
  Claude
    5h: 75% left
    Week: 60% left
  Copilot: github-copilot-usage: malformed JSON response
HTTP paths=["/api/oauth/usage","/copilot_internal/user"]

$ PROOF_MODE=malformed node proof-copilot-cli.mjs  # exact HEAD c4ba6cc7ddc9
mode=malformed CLI exit=0
  Claude
    5h: 75% left
    Week: 60% left
  Copilot: Copilot returned unreadable usage data. Try refreshing usage later.
HTTP paths=["/api/oauth/usage","/copilot_internal/user"]

$ PROOF_MODE=valid PROOF_FORCE_BUILD=0 node proof-copilot-cli.mjs  # exact HEAD negative control
mode=valid CLI exit=0
  Claude
    5h: 75% left
    Week: 60% left
  Copilot (business)
    Premium: 25% left
    Chat: 75% left
HTTP paths=["/api/oauth/usage","/copilot_internal/user"]
```

<details>
<summary>proof-copilot-cli.mjs (save at repository root)</summary>

```js
import assert from "node:assert/strict";
import { spawn } from "node:child_process";
import { mkdtemp, mkdir, writeFile, rm } from "node:fs/promises";
import { createServer } from "node:http";
import { tmpdir } from "node:os";
import { join } from "node:path";

// Run from the repository root. Only remote HTTP requests are redirected;
// CLI, auth store, plugin discovery, usage aggregation and rendering are production code.
const root = await mkdtemp(join(tmpdir(), "copilot-cli-proof-"));
const state = join(root, "state");
const agent = join(state, "agents", "main", "agent");
const mode = process.env.PROOF_MODE ?? "malformed";
assert(["malformed", "valid"].includes(mode));
const requests = [];
const server = createServer((req, res) => {
  requests.push(req.url);
  res.setHeader("Content-Type", "application/json");
  if (req.url === "/copilot_internal/user") {
    res.end(mode === "malformed" ? "{" : JSON.stringify({
      copilot_plan: "business",
      quota_snapshots: {
        premium_interactions: { percent_remaining: 25 },
        chat: { percent_remaining: 75 },
      },
    }));
  } else if (req.url === "/api/oauth/usage") {
    res.end(JSON.stringify({ five_hour: { utilization: 25 }, seven_day: { utilization: 40 } }));
  } else {
    res.writeHead(404);
    res.end("unexpected fixture path");
  }
});
await new Promise((resolve) => server.listen(0, "127.0.0.1", resolve));
const origin = `http://127.0.0.1:${server.address().port}`;
const preload = join(root, "network.mjs");
await mkdir(agent, { recursive: true });
await writeFile(join(state, "openclaw.json"), JSON.stringify({
  agents: {
    ownership: "explicit",
    defaults: { systemAgent: { agentId: "main" }, model: { primary: "github-copilot/gpt-4o" } },
    entries: { main: {} },
  },
  plugins: { allow: ["github-copilot", "anthropic"] },
  gateway: { mode: "local", port: 19879, auth: { mode: "token", token: "fixture-gateway-token" } },
}));
await writeFile(preload, `
const originalFetch = globalThis.fetch;
globalThis.fetch = (input, init) => {
  const request = new Request(input, init);
  const url = new URL(request.url);
  if (!["api.github.com", "api.anthropic.com"].includes(url.hostname)) {
    throw new Error("Proof forbids non-fixture HTTP: " + url.hostname);
  }
  return originalFetch(new Request(${JSON.stringify(origin)} + url.pathname + url.search, request));
};
`);
const env = {
  PATH: process.env.PATH,
  HOME: root,
  LANG: "en_US.UTF-8",
  OPENCLAW_STATE_DIR: state,
  OPENCLAW_CONFIG_PATH: join(state, "openclaw.json"),
  OPENCLAW_DEV_SOURCE_ROOT: process.cwd(),
  OPENCLAW_NO_RESPAWN: "1",
  OPENCLAW_FORCE_BUILD: process.env.PROOF_FORCE_BUILD ?? "1",
  NO_COLOR: "1",
  npm_config_manage_package_manager_versions: "false",
  NODE_OPTIONS: `--import=${preload}`,
};
async function run(command, args) {
  return await new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const child = spawn(command, args, { env, cwd: process.cwd(), stdio: ["ignore", "pipe", "pipe"] });
    let stdout = "", stderr = "";
    child.stdout.on("data", (data) => { stdout += data; });
    child.stderr.on("data", (data) => { stderr += data; });
    child.on("error", reject);
    child.on("close", (code) => resolve({ code, stdout, stderr }));
  });
}
try {
  const setup = await run(process.execPath, ["--import", "./scripts/tsx.mjs", "--input-type=module", "-e", `
    import { upsertAuthProfile } from "./src/agents/auth-profiles.ts";
    for (const provider of ["github-copilot", "anthropic"]) {
      upsertAuthProfile({ agentDir: ${JSON.stringify(agent)}, profileId: provider + ":fixture",
        credential: { type: "token", provider, token: "fixture-token", expires: 4102444800000 } });
    }
  `]);
  assert.equal(setup.code, 0, setup.stderr);
  const cli = await run("pnpm", ["openclaw", "status", "--usage", "--timeout", "15000"]);
  console.log(`mode=${mode} CLI exit=${cli.code}`);
  // Print the unchanged CLI usage lines, rather than reconstructed snapshots.
  console.log(cli.stdout.split("\n").filter((line) => /^  (Copilot|Claude)|^\s+(5h:|Week:|Premium:|Chat:)/.test(line)).join("\n"));
  console.log("HTTP paths=" + JSON.stringify(requests));
  assert.equal(cli.code, 0, cli.stderr);
  assert(requests.includes("/copilot_internal/user"), cli.stdout + cli.stderr);
  assert(requests.includes("/api/oauth/usage"), cli.stdout + cli.stderr);
  assert(cli.stdout.includes("Claude"), cli.stdout);
  assert(cli.stdout.includes("75% left"), cli.stdout);
  const expectedCopilotLine = process.env.PROOF_EXPECT_COPILOT_LINE ?? (mode === "malformed"
    ? "Copilot: Copilot returned unreadable usage data. Try refreshing usage later."
    : "Copilot (business)");
  assert(cli.stdout.includes(expectedCopilotLine), cli.stdout);
} finally {
  server.closeAllConnections();
  await new Promise((resolve) => server.close(resolve));
  await rm(root, { recursive: true, force: true });
}
```

</details>

