## Security-owner decision: metadata classification

**Acceptance requested for tested head `60607a33ba6948cb88ead4f168e2dd6424078941`; acceptance is not yet recorded.** This is a credential-classification decision, not a claim that all metadata is public or free of privacy implications.

### Exact exemption

Only these six header names are recognized (case-insensitive after normalization). An otherwise unclassified value is passed through without sentinel sealing or automatic known-secret registration at this boundary:

| Header | Defined role | Scope of the requested acceptance |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Accept` | Response media-type preferences | Do not classify normal negotiation values as credentials. |
| `Accept-Encoding` | Acceptable response content codings | Preserve ordinary values such as `identity`. |
| `Accept-Language` | Response language preferences | Keep negotiation data out of credential registration; language preferences can still be privacy-sensitive. |
| `Content-Type` | Representation media type | Preserve ordinary media types such as `application/json`. |
| `User-Agent` | Client product information | Do not automatically classify product/version text as credentials; it can still identify or fingerprint a client. |
| `OpenAI-Organization` | Organization selection for an authenticated API request | Treat the identifier as account/routing metadata, not an authentication credential; explicitly accept the privacy tradeoff below. |

The five HTTP-field roles follow [RFC 9110](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html), especially sections 8.3, 10.1.5 and 12.5. OpenAI documents bearer credentials separately from its organization-selection header in its [authentication reference](https://developers.openai.com/api/reference/overview#authentication). That separation supports the proposed classification; it does **not** establish that an organization identifier is safe to disclose publicly.

### Protection takes precedence

1. Nonempty values other than recognized non-secret markers, identified from API keys, explicit bearer/header auth, and non-exempt headers in the same prepared result are protected before metadata aliases are finalized. Exact duplicate values remain on the credential-protection path regardless of the logging registry’s minimum length, capacity, or header insertion order. Existing non-secret marker semantics remain unchanged.
2. An explicit header-auth declaration protects that header even when its name appears in the exemption list. Already registered exact secret values retain their existing protection behavior. Sentinel-shaped values are preserved as-is by the preparer; egress validation is unchanged.
3. Unknown/custom header names remain on the protection path. The six-name list is not an inversion of a sensitive-name heuristic and does not exempt all provider metadata.

Sealing still follows the existing `OPENCLAW_SECRET_SENTINELS` mode. With sealing disabled, raw values remain raw; values below the unchanged six-character redaction minimum are not registered. This repair does not override that existing opt-out or claim universal sealing/masking.

### Deliberate tradeoff and limits

This is a **header-name policy with credential-precedence checks**, not arbitrary value-content validation. Metadata without that provenance can remain plaintext in retained runtime state, and matching ordinary text will no longer be automatically masked merely because the provider returned the same value. Independent pattern redaction and other existing controls are not disabled. The exact-value duplicate checks are not a general detector for credentials embedded inside differently formatted metadata.

Organization identifiers may reveal account affiliation; language and product metadata can also carry privacy information ([RFC 9110 privacy discussion](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#section-17.13)). The proposed policy distinguishes credential protection from general metadata confidentiality. It adds no new logger or network recipient, but intentionally removes automatic sealing/registration for unclassified values under these six names. It does not provide a new general metadata-confidentiality setting, restore already-masked history, or clear an existing process registry.

### Concrete owner decision

Please either **accept the six exemptions with the precedence and privacy limits above**, including `OpenAI-Organization`, or identify the specific header(s) that must remain protected so the exemption can be narrowed accordingly. Removing a disputed header would be a follow-up code/proof change, not an assumption that owner acceptance is unnecessary for the remaining classification change. No owner approval, review waiver, or resolution of this merge gate is implied by the tests or bot rating.

